    "q-350328": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350328",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-2,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope $m=-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+2) \\\\ y & =-x-2+1 \\\\ y & =-x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=-x-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
