    "q-350373": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350373",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t,\\,y=4{t}^{2}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-12t+12,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12t+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{(t-2)}^{2} & =0\\,⟹\\,t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =8(2)-3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
