    "q-122836": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122836",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal.",
      "worked_solution": "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4t({t}^{2}+1)=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=3(0)^{2}-3=-3\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
